# Erdos Problem #99 - weighted outer-flex floor and near-equality classification

**Date:** 2026-09-14
**Target:** C-II, canonical full-core Type-II branch
**Dependencies:** minimax phase-star atomic gap `g=0.0021`; actual-outer/flat-pentagon endpoint gate; audited Section 156 train-angle closure coordinates; flat-pentagon three-phase stability
**Claim class:** proof-only structural theorem awaiting independent audit

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The outer-supported private-kernel argument should be combined with the weighted endpoint gate before splitting into an outer-to-outer-rich or outer-to-flat-rich branch. Doing so gives a substantially stronger universal structured-flex floor and, more importantly, a sharp stability description of near equality.

Let

- `L_OO` be the number of complete trains with both terminal occurrences on the actual outer face;
- `L_OF` count complete trains with one actual-outer terminal and one terminal on a metrically good flat pentagon;
- `r_P` be the number of such outer-to-flat trains ending on a good pentagon `P`;
- `G` be the number of good pentagons receiving at least one such train; and
- `p:=sum_P(r_P-2)_+`.

The exact closure-coordinate argument gives an outer-supported kernel

\[
\boxed{\dim K_{out}\ge L_{OO}+p.}
\tag{WF.1}
\]

After quotienting global rotation, the non-Euclidean contact-flex dimension loses at most one, irrelevant at `D` scale.

On an `R_F=o(D)` Type-II subsequence write

\[
t={q-k\over D}\to t,\qquad
\delta={b-P_F\over D}\to\delta,\qquad
\kappa={k\over D}\to\kappa.
\]

Put

\[
s=\sqrt{\Delta_2},\qquad \Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},\qquad
g=0.0021,\qquad C_g=2s+g.
\]

The actual-outer endpoint gate and the current atomic theorem give

\[
{2L_{OO}+L_{OF}\over D}
\ge R(t,\delta,\kappa)-o(1),
\tag{WF.2}
\]

where

\[
R(t,\delta,\kappa)
:=7\pi+3C_g-{27t\over2}+7\delta+{3\kappa\over2}.
\tag{WF.3}
\]

The exact outer ledger gives

\[
{Q\over D}=q_0(t,\delta)+o(1),
\qquad
q_0(t,\delta):=2t-\pi-\delta,
\tag{WF.4}
\]

and `G<=Q`.

The sharp weighted consequence is

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\dim K_{out}\over D}
\ge F(t,\delta,\kappa),
}
\tag{WF.5}
\]

with

\[
\boxed{
F(t,\delta,\kappa)
:={R-2q_0\over2}
={9\pi\over2}+{3C_g\over2}-{35t\over4}
+{9\delta\over2}+{3\kappa\over4}.
}
\tag{WF.6}
\]

At the safe current endpoint

\[
t_{21}={4\over9}(\pi+s)+0.0017279,
\]

and after dropping the nonnegative bonuses,

\[
\boxed{F(t_{21},0,0)=1.061392779\ldots .}
\tag{WF.7}
\]

This is almost twice the `0.56049D` floor obtained by first replacing `L_OO+L_OF` with half of `2L_OO+L_OF`.

The same algebra has a useful stability statement. Let `N_{=2}` be the number of good pentagons carrying exactly two outer-to-flat trains. If along a subsequence

\[
{\dim K_{out}\over D}
\le F(t,\delta,\kappa)+\eta+o(1),
\tag{WF.8}
\]

then

\[
\boxed{{p\over D}\le2\eta+o(1),}
\tag{WF.9}
\]

\[
\boxed{{L_{OO}\over D}\ge F(t,\delta,\kappa)-\eta-o(1),}
\tag{WF.10}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{{N_{=2}\over D}\ge q_0(t,\delta)-2\eta-o(1).}
\tag{WF.11}
\]

The two trains belonging to different `N_{=2}` faces are automatically distinct. On the slow flatness scale, their projective axes are either `o(1)` apart or `pi/3+o(1)` apart. Pigeonholing the two types therefore gives a pairwise train-disjoint coherent hinge family of size

\[
\boxed{
{H_{coh}\over D}
\ge {q_0(t,\delta)\over2}-\eta-o(1).
}
\tag{WF.12}
\]

Thus the sharp flex floor has a rigid near-equality endpoint: either the localized flex space is larger than its scalar minimum, or essentially every exceptional-face slot is occupied by an exact two-outer-train flat pentagon while more than one full `D` of outer-to-outer train shears coexist with it.

## 1. Exact private-column dimension

Every outer-to-outer train is a zero column of `C_face`, giving `L_OO` independent train-angle kernel coordinates. For a good pentagon `P`, its `r_P` outer-to-flat train columns occur only in the two closure rows of `P`, so their local kernel dimension is at least `(r_P-2)_+`. Different pentagons use disjoint outer-to-flat train sets, and those sets are disjoint from outer-to-outer trains. This proves (WF.1).

## 2. Weighted optimization without branch splitting

Set, after normalization by `D`,

\[
x={L_{OO}\over D},\qquad y={L_{OF}\over D},\qquad \rho={p\over D},\qquad q={Q\over D}.
\]

Write

\[
y=s_2+\rho,
\qquad
s_2:={1\over D}\sum_P\min(r_P,2).
\tag{WF.13}
\]

Since `G<=Q`,

\[
s_2\le {2G\over D}\le2q.
\tag{WF.14}
\]

The endpoint gate is

\[
2x+y\ge R-o(1).
\]

Therefore

\[
\begin{aligned}
2(x+\rho)
&=(2x+y)+(\rho-s_2)\\
&\ge R-2q+\rho-o(1).
\end{aligned}
\tag{WF.15}
\]

Dropping the nonnegative `rho/2` term proves

\[
x+\rho\ge {R-2q\over2}-o(1),
\]

which is (WF.5)--(WF.6). This is the exact scalar minimization; no case split on `L_OO` versus `L_OF` is required.

Equation (WF.15) actually records more:

\[
\boxed{x+\rho\ge F+{\rho\over2}-o(1).}
\tag{WF.16}
\]

This is the source of near-equality rigidity.

## 3. Stability of the private excess

Assume (WF.8). Combining it with (WF.16) gives

\[
\rho\le2\eta+o(1),
\]

which proves (WF.9).

Also `y=s_2+rho<=2q+rho`. From the endpoint gate,

\[
2x\ge R-y-o(1)\ge R-2q-\rho-o(1)=2F-\rho-o(1).
\]

Hence

\[
x\ge F-\rho/2-o(1)\ge F-\eta-o(1),
\]

which proves (WF.10).

## 4. Exact-two-face stability

The endpoint gate and the upper bound in (WF.8) also imply a lower bound on `s_2`. Since `x+rho<=F+eta+o(1)`,

\[
x\le F+\eta-\rho+o(1).
\]

Substitute in `2x+s_2+rho>=R-o(1)` and use `2F=R-2q`:

\[
s_2\ge2q-2\eta+\rho-o(1).
\tag{WF.17}
\]

Let `N_{>=2}` be the number of good pentagons with `r_P>=2` and `N_{>=3}` the number with `r_P>=3`. Since

\[
s_2=N_1+2N_{>=2},
\qquad
G=N_0+N_1+N_{>=2},
\]

we have

\[
N_{>=2}\ge s_2-G.
\]

Using `G<=qD+o(D)` and (WF.17),

\[
{N_{>=2}\over D}\ge q-2\eta+\rho-o(1).
\tag{WF.18}
\]

Each face counted by `N_{>=3}` contributes at least one to `p`, so

\[
{N_{>=3}\over D}\le\rho.
\]

Therefore

\[
{N_{=2}\over D}
={N_{>=2}-N_{>=3}\over D}
\ge q-2\eta-o(1),
\]

proving (WF.11).

## 5. Coherent hinge extraction

Each face counted by `N_{=2}` has exactly two outer-to-flat terminal trains. Different faces use disjoint train pairs because an outer-to-flat train has only one bounded endpoint.

Flat-pentagon stability places all five terminal train axes in three shrinking projective neighborhoods of the triangular-lattice phases. Thus the unique pair at each `N_{=2}` face has projective separation either `o(1)` or `pi/3+o(1)`. Split the faces into these two classes. One class has at least half the faces, proving (WF.12).

No crossing between the two trains is asserted. The theorem records only their exact common bounded endpoint and asymptotic phase relation.

## 6. Numerical endpoint

At `t=t_21`, `delta=kappa=0`, the companion certificate gives

\[
q_0=0.500890222369096\ldots,
\]

\[
R=3.124565998763791\ldots,
\]

\[
F=1.061392779012799\ldots .
\]

For example, choose `eta=0.005`. Then every low-t, small-residual survivor satisfies one of:

\[
\boxed{{\dim K_{out}\over D}\ge1.0663927790\ldots-o(1),}
\tag{WF.19}
\]

or simultaneously

\[
\boxed{{L_{OO}\over D}\ge1.0563927790\ldots-o(1),}
\tag{WF.20}
\]

\[
\boxed{{N_{=2}\over D}\ge0.4908902223\ldots-o(1),}
\tag{WF.21}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{{H_{coh}\over D}\ge0.2454451111\ldots-o(1).}
\tag{WF.22}
\]

The example `eta` is arbitrary; (WF.8)--(WF.12) are the theorem.

## 7. Frontier effect

This removes a large amount of combinatorial ambiguity from the outer-supported branch. The sharp low-t endpoint is not merely forced to have a large contact-flex space. Near the smallest possible such space it must have, simultaneously,

```text
~1.06 D actual outer-to-outer train shears,
~0.50 D flat pentagons carrying exactly two outer-to-flat trains,
~0.25 D train-disjoint coherent hinge pairs of one phase type,
and only o(D) private excess beyond two outer trains per face.
```

A closing theorem can now target the diameter-thrackle action on this near-equality model. For an exact-two hinge, the difference of the two outer-train coordinates is the natural local mode; diameter rows that see the two associated cuts identically cancel on that mode. The missing step is a finite circular-order/metric theorem bounding the rank supplied by diameter rows that distinguish linearly many such paired cuts.

This note does not assert that such a rank bound is already known and does not infer a same-diameter motion from the hinge count alone.

## 8. Scope audit

- The theorem is conditional on the existing low-t `R_F=o(D)` actual-outer endpoint gate.
- `p` counts full private-column excess; no one-mode-per-face thinning is used.
- Global rotation can reduce finite non-Euclidean dimension by at most one; all normalized statements absorb this in `o(1)`.
- `G<=Q` is the only face-count upper bound used in the weighted optimization.
- The exact-two conclusion is a stability statement conditional on (WF.8); it is not asserted unconditionally.
- The coherent hinge classification uses only flat-pentagon phase stability; no actual train crossing is inferred.
- Constants use the current independently audited `g=0.0021` theorem and safe `t_21` floor.
- No exact unit triangle or same-cardinality strict sub-D competitor is produced.

**Disposition:** new proof-only sharp weighted floor and near-equality reduction. C-II and Level I remain open.
